    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to identify the position (1-based index) of the single character in a string that differs from all other characters. The input string is guaranteed to contain exactly two distinct lowercase letters, with all but one character being the same.\n\n---\n\n### **Approach**\n\nThe approach is based on determining the **majority character** (the one that appears more than once) and then locating the **single different character**.\n\n1. **Check the first two characters**:\n   - If they are the same, this character is the majority.\n   - Loop through the string to find the first character that differs from this majority and return its 1-based index.\n\n2. **If the first two characters differ**:\n   - The third character will help determine the majority.\n   - If the third character matches the first, the second is the odd one out.\n   - If the third character matches the second, the first is the odd one out.\n\nThis logic ensures that we can handle all valid input cases efficiently and in constant time for the first two characters and linear time for the rest of the string.\n\n---\n\n### **Python Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    s = input().strip()\n    \n    if s[0] == s[1]:\n        majority = s[0]\n        for i, c in enumerate(s):\n            if c != majority:\n                print(i + 1)\n                return\n    else:\n        if s[2] == s[0]:\n            print(2)\n        else:\n            print(1)\n\n# Call the function\nsolve()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation of the Code**\n\n- **Input Handling**: The string is read from standard input and stripped of any leading/trailing whitespace.\n- **Majority Detection**:\n  - If the first two characters are the same, we assume they are part of the majority and scan the string to find the first character that differs.\n- **Edge Case Handling**:\n  - If the first two characters differ, the third character is used to determine which of the first two is the majority, and the other is the single differing character.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample Test Cases**\n\n| Input | Output | Explanation |\n|-------|--------|-------------|\n| `yay` | `2`    | The second character (`a`) differs from the rest (`y`). |\n| `egg` | `1`    | The first character (`e`) differs from the rest (`g`). |\n| `zzzzzwz` | `6` | The sixth character (`w`) differs from the rest (`z`). |\n\nThis approach guarantees correctness and efficiency, adhering strictly to the problem constraints.",
