      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef getSmallestString(s, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the lexicographically smallest string t such that distance(s, t) <= k.\n    The distance is calculated as the sum of cyclic distances between characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    res = list(s)\n    \n    for i in range(len(s)):\n        if k <= 0:\n            break\n            \n        current_char_code = ord(s[i]) - ord('a')\n        \n        # We want to change s[i] to 'a' if possible to make it lexicographically smallest.\n        # The cyclic distance from current_char to 'a' is:\n        # min(distance moving backwards, distance moving forwards)\n        # distance backwards: current_char_code - 0\n        # distance forwards: 26 - current_char_code\n        dist_to_a = min(current_char_code, 26 - current_char_code)\n        \n        if dist_to_a <= k:\n            # We can afford to change this character to 'a'\n            res[i] = 'a'\n            k -= dist_to_a\n        else:\n            # We cannot reach 'a', so we move as close to 'a' as possible \n            # by shifting the character backwards by k units.\n            # Since k < dist_to_a, we just subtract k from the current character code.\n            res[i] = chr(ord(s[i]) - k)\n            k = 0\n            \n    return \"\".join(res)\n```",
