    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of finding the **maximum distance between the indices of two (not necessarily different) prime numbers in a given array**, we can follow a structured and efficient approach.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\n1. **Constraints on Input**:\n   - `1 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^5`\n   - `1 <= nums[i] <= 100`\n   - The array is guaranteed to contain **at least one prime number**.\n\n2. **Efficient Prime Checking**:\n   - Since the values of `nums[i]` are bounded by `100`, we can **precompute all prime numbers up to 100** using the **Sieve of Eratosthenes**.\n   - This allows for **O(1)** prime checks during the array traversal.\n\n3. **Optimal Strategy**:\n   - Traverse the array once to:\n     - Track the **first occurrence** of any prime.\n     - Track the **last occurrence** of any prime.\n   - The **maximum distance** between any two primes in the array will be `last - first`.\n\n4. **Space Efficiency**:\n   - We avoid storing all indices of primes, which would use O(k) space where k is the number of primes in the array.\n   - Instead, we use only two variables (`first`, `last`) to maintain the required information, resulting in **O(1) space**.\n\n---\n\n### Final Python Function\n\n```python\ndef max_distance_primes(nums):\n    # Precompute all primes up to 100 using Sieve of Eratosthenes\n    primes = set()\n    is_prime = [True] * 101\n    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False\n    for i in range(2, 101):\n        if is_prime[i]:\n            primes.add(i)\n            for j in range(i * i, 101, i):\n                is_prime[j] = False\n\n    # Track first and last occurrence of prime numbers\n    first = -1\n    last = -1\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        if num in primes:\n            if first == -1:\n                first = i\n            last = i\n\n    return last - first\n```\n\n---\n\n### Time and Space Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity**:  \n  - Prime sieve: O(100 log log 100) → Constant time  \n  - Array traversal: O(n)  \n  - Total: **O(n)**\n\n- **Space Complexity**:  \n  - Boolean list for sieve: O(101)  \n  - Additional variables: O(1)  \n  - Total: **O(1)** (constant space with respect to input size)\n\n---\n\nThis approach is both **efficient and robust**, and it adheres to the constraints and requirements of the problem.",
