    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef medianOfUniquenessArray(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Total number of subarrays is n * (n + 1) / 2\n    total_subarrays = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    # The median is the ((total_subarrays + 1) // 2)-th smallest element (1-indexed)\n    target = (total_subarrays + 1) // 2\n\n    def count_subarrays_with_distinct_at_most(k):\n        \"\"\"\n        Counts how many subarrays have at most k distinct elements.\n        Uses a sliding window approach.\n        \"\"\"\n        count = 0\n        left = 0\n        freq = {}\n        distinct_count = 0\n        for right in range(n):\n            if nums[right] not in freq or freq[nums[right]] == 0:\n                distinct_count += 1\n            freq[nums[right]] = freq.get(nums[right], 0) + 1\n            \n            while distinct_count > k:\n                freq[nums[left]] -= 1\n                if freq[nums[left]] == 0:\n                    distinct_count -= 1\n                left += 1\n            \n            # All subarrays ending at 'right' starting from 'left' to 'right' \n            # have at most 'k' distinct elements.\n            count += (right - left + 1)\n        return count\n\n    # Binary search for the smallest value 'x' such that \n    # count_subarrays_with_distinct_at_most(x) >= target\n    low = 1\n    high = len(set(nums))\n    ans = high\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if count_subarrays_with_distinct_at_most(mid) >= target:\n            ans = mid\n            high = mid - 1\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n            \n    return ans\n```",
