    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAlex is participating in the filming of another video of BrMeast, and BrMeast asked Alex to prepare 250 thousand tons of TNT, but Alex didn't hear him well, so he prepared $n$ boxes and arranged them in a row waiting for trucks. The $i$-th box from the left weighs $a_i$ tons.\n\nAll trucks that Alex is going to use hold the same number of boxes, denoted by $k$. Loading happens the following way:\n\n \n-  The first $k$ boxes goes to the first truck, \n-  The second $k$ boxes goes to the second truck, \n-  $\\dotsb$ \n-  The last $k$ boxes goes to the $\\frac{{n}}{{k}}$-th truck. Upon loading is completed, each truck must have exactly $k$ boxes. In other words, if at some point it is not possible to load exactly $k$ boxes into the truck, then the loading option with that $k$ is not possible.\n\nAlex hates justice, so he wants the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of two trucks to be as great as possible. If there is only one truck, this value is $0$.\n\nAlex has quite a lot of connections, so for every $1 \\leq k \\leq n$, he can find a company such that each of its trucks can hold exactly $k$ boxes. Print the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of any two trucks.\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains one integer $t$ ($1 \\leq t \\leq 10^4$) — the number of test cases.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer $n$ ($1 \\leq n \\leq 150\\,000$) — the number of boxes.\n\nThe second line contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$ ($1 \\leq a_i \\leq 10^9$) — the weights of the boxes.\n\nIt is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ for all test cases does not exceed $150\\,000$.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, print a single integer — the answer to the problem.Sample Input 1:\n5\n\n2\n\n1 2\n\n6\n\n10 2 3 6 1 3\n\n4\n\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\n15\n\n60978 82265 78961 56708 39846 31071 4913 4769 29092 91348 64119 72421 98405 222 14294\n\n8\n\n19957 69913 37531 96991 57838 21008 14207 19198\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\n1\n9\n0\n189114\n112141\n\n\nNote\n\nIn the first case, we should pick two trucks, so the first one will have only the first box, and the second one will have only the second box.\n\nIn the second case, we should pick six trucks, so the maximum will be $10$, the minimum will be $1$, and the answer is $10 - 1 = 9$.\n\nIn the third case, for any possible $k$, the trucks will have the same total weight of boxes, so the answer is $0$.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of any two trucks when Alex arranges boxes in different configurations. Each configuration is defined by a divisor of the number of boxes, `n`, where each truck must carry exactly `k` boxes. \n\n### Approach\n1. **Input Reading and Prefix Sum Calculation**: For each test case, read the input values and compute the prefix sums of the array to efficiently calculate subarray sums.\n2. **Divisors Generation**: Generate all divisors of `n` using trial division up to the square root of `n`.\n3. **Group Sum Calculation**: For each divisor `k`, compute the total weights of each group of `k` consecutive boxes using the prefix sums.\n4. **Max-Min Difference Calculation**: For each divisor, determine the maximum and minimum group sums and compute their difference, keeping track of the maximum difference encountered.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    ptr = 0\n    t = int(input[ptr])\n    ptr += 1\n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(input[ptr])\n        ptr += 1\n        a = list(map(int, input[ptr:ptr + n]))\n        ptr += n\n        \n        # Compute prefix sums\n        prefix = [0] * (n + 1)\n        for i in range(n):\n            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + a[i]\n        \n        # Generate all divisors of n\n        divisors = set()\n        for i in range(1, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                divisors.add(i)\n                divisors.add(n // i)\n        \n        max_diff = 0\n        \n        for k in divisors:\n            group_count = n // k\n            if group_count == 0:\n                continue\n            \n            # Calculate group sums using prefix sums\n            group_sums = [prefix[i * k] - prefix[(i - 1) * k] for i in range(1, group_count + 1)]\n            current_min = min(group_sums)\n            current_max = max(group_sums)\n            diff = current_max - current_min\n            \n            if diff > max_diff:\n                max_diff = diff\n        \n        print(max_diff)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Prefix Sum Calculation**: The prefix sum array allows us to compute the sum of any subarray in constant time. This is crucial for efficiently determining the total weight of each group of boxes.\n2. **Divisors Generation**: By iterating up to the square root of `n`, we efficiently find all divisors of `n`, ensuring we consider each valid configuration of trucks and boxes.\n3. **Group Sum Calculation**: Using the prefix sums, we compute the sum of each group of `k` boxes in constant time per group, which is efficient and necessary given the problem constraints.\n4. **Max-Min Difference**: For each divisor, after computing all group sums, we determine the maximum and minimum sums and their difference, updating the global maximum difference accordingly.\n\nThis approach ensures that we efficiently handle the constraints and compute the required values in a feasible time complexity.",
