    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can use dynamic programming. We want to find the number of subsets of the set $\\{1^x, 2^x, 3^x, \\dots, k^x\\}$ such that their sum equals $n$. Since $n \\le 300$, we only need to consider integers $i$ such that $i^x \\le n$.\n\nThe DP state `dp[s]` will represent the number of ways to form the sum `s` using a subset of the powers computed so far. We iterate through each power $v = i^x$ (starting from $i=1$ up to $i^x \\le n$) and update the DP table in reverse to ensure each integer is used at most once.\n\n```python\ndef count_ways(n, x):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # dp[i] will store the number of ways to get sum i\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    # Find all powers i^x <= n\n    powers = []\n    i = 1\n    while True:\n        val = i**x\n        if val > n:\n            break\n        powers.append(val)\n        i += 1\n        \n    # Standard 0/1 Knapsack DP\n    # We iterate through each power and update dp table backwards\n    for p in powers:\n        for s in range(n, p - 1, -1):\n            dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - p]) % MOD\n            \n    return dp[n]\n```",
