    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The ellipse $kx^2 + y^2 = 1$ can be written as $\\frac{x^2}{1/k} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1$, so its semi-axes are $a = 1$ (vertical) and $b = 1/\\sqrt{k}$ (horizontal), with $k > 1$ implying $b < a$. The foci lie on the major (vertical) axis at $(0, \\pm c)$, where $c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{1 - 1/k}$. A circle through $F_1 = (0, c)$ and $F_2 = (0, -c)$ and tangent to the ellipse at two points on the $x$-axis must be centered on the $x$-axis (by symmetry), say at $(h, 0)$. Tangency at the $x$-axis implies the ellipse and circle share the same point and derivative there; the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\pm b, 0) = (\\pm 1/\\sqrt{k}, 0)$, so the circle must pass through $(\\pm 1/\\sqrt{k}, 0)$ and have center $(h, 0)$, giving radius $|h \\mp 1/\\sqrt{k}|$. Since it also passes through $(0, \\pm c)$, the distance from $(h, 0)$ to $(0, c)$ equals the radius:  \n$$\nh^2 + c^2 = \\left(h - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)^2.\n$$\nExpanding and simplifying yields $c^2 = \\frac{1}{k} - \\frac{2h}{\\sqrt{k}}$. But $c^2 = 1 - \\frac{1}{k}$, so $1 - \\frac{1}{k} = \\frac{1}{k} - \\frac{2h}{\\sqrt{k}}$, giving $h = \\frac{\\sqrt{k}}{2}\\left(1 - \\frac{2}{k}\\right)$. Since the circle is symmetric about the $y$-axis (foci and tangency points are symmetric), $h = 0$, so $1 - \\frac{2}{k} = 0$, hence $k = 2$.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$",
