      "generated_answer": "The equation of the ellipse is $kx^2 + y^2 = 1$, which can be written as $\\frac{x^2}{1/k} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1$. Since $k > 1$, we have $1/k < 1$, meaning the major axis is along the $y$-axis and the minor axis is along the $x$-axis. The foci are located at $(0, \\pm c)$ where $c^2 = 1 - 1/k$.\n\nThe circle is tangent to the ellipse at two points on the $x$-axis. The vertices of the ellipse on the $x$-axis are $(\\pm 1/\\sqrt{k}, 0)$. Because the circle is tangent to the ellipse at these points, its center must lie on the $y$-axis (due to symmetry) and its radius must be $r = 1/\\sqrt{k}$. Let the center of the circle be $(0, y_0)$. The equation of the circle is $x^2 + (y - y_0)^2 = r^2 = 1/k$.\n\nSince the circle passes through the foci $(0, \\pm c)$, we substitute these coordinates into the circle equation: $0^2 + (\\pm c - y_0)^2 = 1/k$. This gives $(c - y_0)^2 = 1/k$ and $(-c - y_0)^2 = 1/k$, which implies $y_0 = 0$. Thus, $c^2 = 1/k$. Substituting $c^2 = 1 - 1/k$, we get $1 - 1/k = 1/k$, which simplifies to $2/k = 1$, or $k = 2$.\n\n\\boxed{2}",
