    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The inverse Laplace transform of $\\log\\left(\\frac{s+1}{s-1}\\right)$ can be found using the property that $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{f(t)}{t}\\right\\} = \\int_s^\\infty F(u)\\,du$, or by recognizing that $\\log\\left(\\frac{s+1}{s-1}\\right) = \\log(s+1) - \\log(s-1)$, and using known transforms: $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{\\log(s-a)\\} = -\\frac{e^{at}}{t}$ (for $s > a$). Thus,  \n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\log\\left(\\frac{s+1}{s-1}\\right)\\right\\} = -\\frac{e^{-t}}{t} + \\frac{e^{t}}{t} = \\frac{e^t - e^{-t}}{t} = \\frac{2\\sinh t}{t}.\n$$\n\nThe correct answer is **D**.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
