    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10537",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the energy stored in an inductor from t = 0 to t = 1 whose \\textphi -icharacteristic is given by \\textphi (t) = 1/3i^3 (t) and the current through it is given by i (t) = 2 sin t.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.00 J\nB. 0.75 J\nC. 1.50 J\nD. 4.00 J\nE. 2.50 J\nF. 3.00 J\nG. 1.25 J\nH. 2.75 J\nI. 2.00 J\nJ. 3.50 J\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the energy stored in the inductor from $ t = 0 $ to $ t = 1 $, we begin by analyzing the given relationships:\n\n- The flux linkage $ \\phi(t) $ is defined as $ \\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3(t) $,\n- The current through the inductor is $ i(t) = 2 \\sin t $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Energy Formula\n\nThe energy stored in an inductor is generally calculated as:\n\n$$\nW = \\int_0^T v(t)i(t) \\, dt\n$$\n\nwhere $ v(t) $ is the voltage across the inductor. Since the inductor is nonlinear (i.e., $ \\phi $ is not linear in $ i $), the standard formula $ \\frac{1}{2}Li^2 $ does not apply. Instead, the voltage is given by:\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given $ \\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3(t) $, and using $ i(t) = 2 \\sin t $, we compute:\n\n$$\n\\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}(2 \\sin t)^3 = \\frac{8}{3} \\sin^3 t\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\frac{8}{3} \\sin^3 t \\right) = 8 \\sin^2 t \\cos t\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Compute the Power and Integrate\n\nThe instantaneous power is:\n\n$$\np(t) = v(t) \\cdot i(t) = 8 \\sin^2 t \\cos t \\cdot 2 \\sin t = 16 \\sin^3 t \\cos t\n$$\n\nNow, integrate the power over the time interval $ t \\in [0, 1] $:\n\n$$\nW = \\int_0^1 16 \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, dt\n$$\n\nLet $ u = \\sin t $, so $ du = \\cos t \\, dt $, and the limits change from $ t = 0 $ to $ t = 1 $, which corresponds to $ u = 0 $ to $ u = \\sin 1 $.\n\n$$\nW = 16 \\int_0^{\\sin 1} u^3 \\, du = 16 \\left[ \\frac{u^4}{4} \\right]_0^{\\sin 1} = 4 \\sin^4 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Evaluate the Result\n\nUsing $ \\sin(1) \\approx 0.8415 $:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4 1 \\approx (0.8415)^4 \\approx 0.5014\n$$\n\n$$\nW \\approx 4 \\cdot 0.5014 = 2.0056 \\, \\text{J}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately **2.00 J**, which corresponds to **option I**.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{I}\n$$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 221,
        "output_tokens": 5687,
        "total_tokens": 5908
      },
      "provider": "qwen",
      "error": null
