    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nIn the period 1450 to 1750, many West and Central African leaders leveraged the Atlantic trade to exchange enslaved people and resources for European firearms, which they used to expand their military power and consolidate new states like Asante and Dahomey. While the provided text by Walter Rodney emphasizes the exploitative nature of these contacts, historical evidence from this era shows that African elites used the \"gun-slave cycle\" to centralize political authority. Other options are incorrect because the Arab slave trade continued, local industries did not immediately collapse, and population growth was driven more by the introduction of American crops via the Columbian Exchange rather than direct reliance on European merchants for food.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
