      "generated_answer": "The first passage undermines option C by showing that British colonizers were not uniformly eager to impose cultural norms, as Bentinck notes prior caution due to security risks despite moral objections to sati. Instead, intervention depended on power dynamics (\"now that we are supreme\"). Options A and D are supported by the advocacy for prohibition, while B relates more to the second passage.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by analyzing the first passage, where Bentinck expresses moral outrage against sati but highlights past caution due to risks to British security, favoring prohibition only now that supremacy is assured. This challenges the assumption in option C that European colonizers were uniformly eager to force cultural norms, as it reveals decisions were often shaped by strategic considerations rather than consistent zeal. Options A and D align with the passage's support for interference, while B pertains to the unrelated second passage on trade.",
