    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In most U.S. jurisdictions, when the defense of insanity is raised as an *affirmative defense*, the defendant bears the burden of *production* (to introduce sufficient evidence to raise the issue) and often the *burden of persuasion* (to prove insanity by a preponderance of the evidence or clear and convincing evidence, depending on the jurisdiction), but *not* beyond a reasonable doubt (which applies only to the prosecution’s elements of the crime). Option C (“the burden of production”) is the most universally correct minimal requirement, as even jurisdictions requiring only production for the defendant on insanity do so; however, some also require persuasion. Given the phrasing and standard rules, **C** is the safest and most accurate choice.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
