      "generated_answer": "Hart introduces the distinction between \"being obliged\" (coerced by a threat, like a gunman) and \"having an obligation\" (subject to a social rule) to demonstrate that obligations arise from accepted rules, not mere force. This analysis is foundational to his concept of law as a union of primary and secondary rules, directly illuminating what it means to have a rule. Therefore, the distinction's core purpose is to explain the nature of a rule itself.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
