      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **G**. Hart’s distinction between “being obliged” (a psychological or coercive state, e.g., being forced to comply) and “having an obligation” (a normative sense of duty, often grounded in rules) is central to his critique of legal positivism and his analysis of how legal systems function. This distinction clarifies that legal rules can impose obligations (normative duties) even in the absence of actual coercion, thereby separating the concept of law from mere force or moral obligation. While related to broader themes, the primary purpose is to differentiate legal from ethical (or other normative) obligations.",
