    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the immediate (one-step) consequence in propositional logic (PL) of the premises:\n\n**Premises:**\n\n1. $ U \\supset W $  \n2. $ W \\supset (X \\equiv \\neg Y) $  \n3. $ (X \\equiv \\neg Y) \\supset Z $  \n4. $ \\neg Z $\n\n---\n\nWe are given the option of identifying a proposition that follows **directly** from the premises in a single logical step.\n\nLet’s analyze the logical structure:\n\n- Premise 3 is: $ (X \\equiv \\neg Y) \\supset Z $\n- Premise 4 is: $ \\neg Z $\n\nUsing **Modus Tollens** (If $ A \\supset B $ and $ \\neg B $, then $ \\neg A $), we can apply this directly to premises 3 and 4:\n\n- $ (X \\equiv \\neg Y) \\supset Z $\n- $ \\neg Z $\n\nThis yields:  \n- $ \\neg (X \\equiv \\neg Y) $\n\nThis is **exactly option D**.\n\n---\n\nOther options require **more than one step** to derive:\n\n- **Option A** (U ⊃ Z): Requires chaining multiple implications, not immediate.\n- **Option B** (U ∨ W): Not logically entailed by the premises in one step.\n- **Option C** (~W): Requires first deriving $ \\neg (X \\equiv \\neg Y) $, then using $ W \\supset (X \\equiv \\neg Y) $, which is a second step.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{D}\n$$",
