      "generated_answer": "Let the cut occur at position $ x $ meters from one end, where $ 0 < x < 12 $. The longer piece has length $ \\max(x, 12 - x) $, which is $ > 6 $ (since the longer piece must be at least half the wire). The square’s perimeter is this longer piece, so its side is $ \\frac{\\max(x, 12 - x)}{4} $, and its area is $ \\left( \\frac{\\max(x, 12 - x)}{4} \\right)^2 $. We want this area $ > 4 $, so $ \\left( \\frac{L}{4} \\right)^2 > 4 \\Rightarrow L > 8 $. Thus, the longer piece must exceed 8 meters. This happens when $ x < 4 $ or $ x > 8 $, giving total favorable length $ 4 + 4 = 8 $ out of total possible cut positions $ 12 $, so probability $ \\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3} $.  \n$$\n\\boxed{B}\n$$",
