      "generated_answer": "Let $X$ be the number of investments that become worthless; $X \\sim \\text{Binomial}(n=3, p=0.2)$. Simone loses no more than $1/3$ of her original investment if at most 1 investment becomes worthless (since losing 2 or 3 would mean losing $2k$ or $3k$, i.e., $2/3$ or $100\\%$ of her total $3k$). So we compute $P(X \\le 1) = P(X=0) + P(X=1) = (0.8)^3 + 3(0.2)(0.8)^2 = 0.512 + 0.384 = 0.896 \\approx 90\\%$.  \nThe closest option is **A. 90%**.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A}\n$$",
