      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we must analyze the structural intervals of the Phrygian, Dorian, and Locrian modes:\n\n1.  **Phrygian mode:** Built on the III degree of a major scale. If **D** is the II degree, the root is C (I), meaning the Phrygian mode would start on E. If **♯F** is the VI degree, the root (I) is A, making the Phrygian mode start on C♯.\n2.  **Dorian mode:** Built on the II degree. It does not typically utilize a \"leading tone\" (major 7th) in its natural form, as it features a minor 7th. \n3.  **Locrian mode:** Built on the VII degree. In this mode, the interval from the tonic to the fifth degree is a diminished fifth (tritone). Therefore, if the tonic is Ti, the fifth degree is **Sol**, which functions as the \"dominant\" position in the scale's structure.\n\nBased on modal theory, Option D correctly identifies a characteristic of the Locrian mode.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
