      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe whole-tone scale consists of six notes spaced by major seconds (2 semitones), yielding intervals of 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 semitones within an octave (major second, major third, augmented fourth, minor sixth, minor seventh). It does not contain a major sixth (9 semitones) or major seventh (11 semitones), making option D incorrect. Options A–C are accurate.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by verifying each option against the properties of the whole-tone scale, which consists of six notes per octave with each step being a major second (2 semitones), resulting in note positions at 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 semitones relative to the root.\n\nFor option A, the interval between adjacent notes is indeed a major second, confirming its accuracy.\n\nOption B is correct, as Debussy's \"Voiles\" prominently features the whole-tone scale.\n\nOption C holds true: the whole-tone scale containing B♭ (e.g., B♭-C-D-E-F-G) necessarily includes E, as it follows the sequential whole steps.\n\nOption D is incorrect because the scale's intervals are limited to 2 (major second), 4 (major third), 6 (augmented fourth), 8 (minor sixth), and 10 (minor seventh) semitones; it lacks a major sixth (9 semitones) and major seventh (11 semitones), as all intervals are even multiples of 2 semitones up to 10.",
