    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following chords belongs to the first inversion of a major-minor seventh chord (including close and open position)? \n \"A\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [F,B,DG]4 |] %1\", \"B\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [A,_EFc]4 |] %1\", \"C\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [^^F,^A,^C^D]4 |] %1\", \"D\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [C_EG_A]4 |] %1\"\n\nOptions: \nA. AB\nB. BC\nC. CD\nD. AD\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The chord in option B ([A,_EFc]) is A3–Eb4–F4–C5, the first inversion of F7 (bass A = major 3rd, with perfect 5th C, minor 7th Eb, root F).  \nOption A is 3rd inversion G7 (bass F3 = ♭7th).  \nOptions C and D do not form a major-minor 7th chord.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying a major-minor seventh chord as one with a major triad (root, major 3rd, perfect 5th) and minor 7th (♭7), with first inversion featuring the major 3rd in the bass. ABC notation is parsed by processing notes sequentially, where each letter represents a pitch (uppercase for C4–B4 range), accidentals (^ for sharp, _ for flat) precede the note, and commas (,) following a note lower its octave by one.  \n\nFor option B ([A,_EFc]), the notes parse as A, (A3), _E (Eb4), F (F4), and c (C5), yielding bass A3 (major 3rd of F), followed by Eb4 (♭7 of F), F4 (root), and C5 (5th), confirming F7 in first inversion (3–♭7–1–5 voicing).  \n\nOption A ([F,B,DG]) parses as F, (F3), B, (B3), D (D4), G (G4), forming G7 notes but with bass F3 (♭7), indicating third inversion. Option C ([^^F,^A,^C^D]) yields non-standard pitches (e.g., ^^F, = F##3 ≈ G3, ^A, = A#3) not matching a major-minor 7th. Option D ([C_EG_A]) is C4–Eb4–G4–Ab4, lacking correct intervals for any major-minor 7th in first inversion. Thus, only B fits the criteria.",
