    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_208",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following chords belongs to the first inversion of a major-minor seventh chord (including close and open position)? \n \"A\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [F,B,DG]4 |] %1\", \"B\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [A,_EFc]4 |] %1\", \"C\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [^^F,^A,^C^D]4 |] %1\", \"D\": \"L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n [C_EG_A]4 |] %1\"\n\nOptions: \nA. AB\nB. BC\nC. CD\nD. AD\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for a chord in first inversion of a **major-minor seventh chord** (i.e., a dominant seventh chord: root, major third, perfect fifth, minor seventh), in first inversion (third in the bass). In key C, a major-minor seventh chord built on G is G–B–D–F (G7), and its first inversion has B in the bass: B–D–F–G (or any voicing with B lowest).  \n\nOption A: `[F,B,DG]4` — notes F, B, D, G ⇒ B is not lowest (F is), and it's missing G as bass; but B is present, and the notes match G7 in close position with F lowest (third inversion).  \nOption B: `[A,_EFc]4` — notes A, E♭, F, c (C5) — includes A, E♭, F, C — this is an A diminished seventh (A–C–E♭–G), not a major-minor seventh.  \nOption C: `[^^F,^A,^C^D]4` — in ABC, `^^F` is F♯, `^A` is A, `^C` is C, `^D` is D (maybe D4 or D5). So F♯–A–C–D — that’s an A minor seventh chord (A–C–E–G) but with F♯ and D instead — actually A minor 7 is A–C–E–G; F♯–A–C–D is not standard.  \nOption D: `[C_EG_A]4` — C, E♭, G, A — notes: C, E♭, G, A — this is C minor 7 (C–E♭–G–B♭) but with A instead of B♭ — not correct.  \n\nWait — reconsider: A major-minor seventh chord is defined as root–major 3rd–perfect 5th–minor 7th. In key C, the only diatonic major-minor seventh chord is G7 = G–B–D–F. Its first inversion is B–D–F–G (B in bass). Let’s check voicings:  \nOption A: `[F,B,DG]` — F, B, D, G — yes, notes of G7, but F is lowest — that’s **third inversion** (minor 7th in bass).  \nOption B: `[A,_EFc]` — A, E♭, F, c (C4 or C5?) — A is root, E♭ is minor 3rd, F is ♭5, c is minor 7th? That's A half-diminished 7 (A–C–E♭–G), but F instead of G — no.  \nOption C: `[^^F,^A,^C^D]` — F♯, A, C, D — this is D minor 7? D–F♯–A–C — yes! D minor 7 is D–F♯–A–C — a major-minor seventh chord (major triad? No: D–F♯–A is major triad, with minor 7th C — yes, Dm7 is a major-minor seventh chord). Its first inversion is F♯–A–C–D — exactly option C! So **C is correct**.  \nOption D: `[C_EG_A]` — C, E♭, G, A — not a standard 7th chord.  \n\nThus, option C (`[^^F,^A,^C^D]4`) = F♯–A–C–D = Dm7 in first inversion (F♯ lowest).  \nAnswer: **C**\n\n\\boxed{C}",
