    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_248",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which type of chord has all of its inversions being equivalent chords?\n\nOptions: \nA. Minor triad\nB. Diminished triad\nC. Half-diminished seventh chord\nD. Fully diminished seventh chord\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The fully diminished seventh chord is constructed entirely from stacked minor thirds (i.e., it divides the octave into four equal parts). Consequently, any inversion simply yields another diminished-seventh chord whose pitch-class content is identical (modulo enharmonic spelling) to the original chord; all four possible roots therefore produce equivalent sonorities.  \n\nNo other option possesses this symmetry: minor and diminished triads invert into chords of a different quality or root, while a half-diminished seventh inverts into a different half-diminished or minor-minor seventh sonority.  \n\nThus the only chord type whose every inversion is an equivalent chord is the fully diminished seventh.  \n\n\\boxed{D}",
