 read by a high ranking British officer. He is amused by Talleyrand's role as Bonaparte's advisor and ĂŠminence grise, a role that he finds plausible. However, he is especially puzzled by references to a British general named \"Wellington.\" In the final line of the story, the British officer is revealed to be Sir Arthur Wellesley - known in our reality as the Duke of Wellington. He attained the title by way of his victories in the Napoleonic Wars, which never took place in this universe.\n\nQuestion: How did Benedict Arnold's death change history?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Benedict Arnold’s death—instead of being merely wounded—led to British General John Burgoyne’s victory over Horatio Gates at the Battles of Saratoga in 1777. This outcome caused the American Revolution to fail, as the decisive colonial defeat discouraged French support and undermined the rebellion’s momentum. Consequently, the French Revolution also failed, the Napoleonic Wars never occurred, and the geopolitical landscape remained under British dominance.  \n\n\\boxed{\\text{Benedict Arnold's death led to a British victory at Saratoga, causing the American and French Revolutions to fail and preventing the Napoleonic Wars.}}",
