    "global index": "QANTA_Fine Arts_1212",
    "prompt": "Please read the following question and provide the correct answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Jean Dauberval (\"doe\"-ber-vahl) was known for rehabilitating the \"false\" examples of these techniques, which were reversals of the \"true\" or \"noble\" ones and generally thought to be only suitable for depictions of drunks and sailors.\n\nProvide the correct answer in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct and the most common answer to the question. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Jean Dauberval was an 18th-century French ballet master best known for works such as La Fille mal gardée that introduced realistic, demi-caractère characters. Historical treatises on noble dance distinguish “true” (noble) cabrioles—beaten leaps performed with the working leg opening forward or to the side—from their “false” reversals, which open in the opposite direction and were long dismissed as vulgar. Dauberval’s choreography rehabilitated the latter steps, making them acceptable for portraying sailors, peasants, and inebriates on stage.\n\nThus the techniques referenced are cabrioles.\n\n\\boxed{cabrioles (caprioles)}",
      "success": true,
      "provider": "nadir",
      "token_usage": {}
    },
    "cost": 0.0,
    "accuracy": 0.0,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
